Q: Converting a hex string to a byte array A: <p>I found most of the answers before this one to be more complicated than necessary for such a simple question. I believe it can be done as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Hex to bytes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>slice the hex string into two-character chunks.</li>
<li>convert each chunk to a <code>base-16</code> numerical value using <code>strtoul</code>.</li>
<li>store each resulting number in a byte vector.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Bytes to hex:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>iterate through the byte vector.</li>
<li>convert each byte to a two-character hex string using a formatter like <code>snprintf</code>.</li>
<li>concatenate the strings to form the final hex representation.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>implementation:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;cstdlib&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

using u8 = unsigned char;

std::vector&lt;u8&gt; hexToBytes(const std::string&amp; hex)
{
  std::vector&lt;u8&gt; buf {};

  u8 val {};
  for (int i = 0; i != hex.length(); i += 2) {
    char st[] = { hex[i], hex[i + 1], 0 };

    char* end;
    val = static_cast&lt;u8&gt;(strtoul(st, &amp;end, 16));
    buf.push_back(val);
  }

  return buf;
}

std::string bytesToHex(const std::vector&lt;u8&gt;&amp; data)
{
  std::string res {};
  char s[3] {};
  for (u8 byte : data) {
    snprintf(s, sizeof(s), &quot;%02hhx&quot;, byte);
    res.append(s);
  }
  return res;
}

/*
        you can test it like so:
*/

int main(int argc, char const* argv[])
{
  // test 1: Hex to Bytes
  std::string hexInput = &quot;48656c6c6f&quot;; // &quot;Hello&quot; in hex
  std::vector&lt;u8&gt; bytes = hexToBytes(hexInput);

  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;hex input: &quot; &lt;&lt; hexInput &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;decoded chars: &quot;;
  for (u8 b : bytes) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; (char)b;
  }
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n\n&quot;;

  // test 2: Bytes to Hex
  std::vector&lt;u8&gt; dataToEncode = { 0xde, 0xad, 0xbe, 0xef, 0x01 };
  std::string hexOutput = bytesToHex(dataToEncode);

  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;byte input: { 0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, 0xEF, 0x01 }&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;encoded hex: &quot; &lt;&lt; hexOutput &lt;&lt; std::endl;

  // validation
  if (hexToBytes(bytesToHex(dataToEncode)) == dataToEncode) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\ntest passed!&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
  }

  return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><em>done, profit</em>!</p>

Q: decodeStream returns null A: <p>You needn't create a new input stream (like @Ponmalar suggested).</p>
<p>There are implementations of <code>InputStream</code> that allow for 'rewinding' of previously read bytes, i. e. their implementation of <code>InputStream</code>'s method <code>markSupported()</code> always returns true.</p>
<p>One of such streams is <code>BufferedInputStream</code> (class hierarchy here: <em>InputStream → FilterInputStream → BufferedInputStream</em>). You can wrap your input stream with <code>BufferedInputStream</code> and use <code>mark()</code> and <code>reset()</code> later on. You may use the following template:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>val bis = BufferedInputStream(inputStream)
// the buffered input stream will remember the position it was in upon calling mark()
bis.mark(Integer.MAX_VALUE)
// call decodeStream() to only decode bounds for now
BitmapFactory.decodeStream(bis, null, opts)
val inSampleSize = calculateInSampleSize(...)
opts.inSampleSize = inSampleSize
opts.inJustDecodeBounds = false
// now you're ready to do the actual sampling of the image;
// reposition the input stream to its beginning
bis.reset() // the position of the input stream is now where it was when you last called mark()
val bitmap = BitmapFactory.decodeStream(bis, null, opts) // the input stream is read again from the beginning
</code></pre>

